</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Four examples of edit detection statistics under the two combinatorial patterns. Each example shows the prompt text, the watermarked LLM-generated text, and the edited text. The detection threshold is marked in red, and detected edit spans are represented by red bars that fall below the threshold. We mark the true detection and missed detection in the plot, under a tolerance of <math id="S4.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=3</annotation></semantics></math>. The examples are generated using LLaMA-2-7b with watermarking strength <math id="S4.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=5.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=5.8</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01637v1/Figures/15_opt-1.3b_5.8.pkl.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:448/389;" width="448" height="389" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01637v1/Figures/15_Llama-2-7b-hf_5.8.pkl.png" id="S4.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:448/389;" width="448" height="389" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01637v1/Figures/15_legend.png" id="S4.F6.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/46;" width="476" height="46" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Edit detection accuracy under different edit lengths (1 to 6 tokens) and three edit types (insertion, replacement, and deletion) on OPT-1.3b (left) and Llama-2-7b (right). The watermarking strength parameter is <math id="S4.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=5.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=5.8</annotation></semantics></math>. In all cases we allow an evaluation tolerance of <math id="S4.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=3</annotation></semantics></math> tokens.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Results on Post-Generation Edit Detection</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first evaluate the performance of our detection method across three canonical types of post-generation edits: replacement, insertion, and deletion. We demonstrate that the pattern-based watermark allows accurate localization of the edited spans.
For each type of edit, Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the edit detection statistics across token positions. It can be seen that the edit detection statistics fall below the threshold in almost all edited tokens, indicating efficient detection of local edits. In contrast, the edit detection statistics lie above the threshold during most non-edit regions, as the threshold is calibrated to achieve a small Type-I error rate. Furthermore, comparing Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (b) and (d), we observe that the longer combinatorial pattern in (d) is more effective at detecting certain edit lengths, particularly in the case of deletions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we compute the average edit detection accuracy over 1,000 samples of 64 tokens long, using a fixed Type-I error rate of 0.1 and a tolerance parameter <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=3</annotation></semantics></math>. Results for both LLaMA-2-7b and OPT-1.3b are shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, evaluated under various combinatorial patterns with a fixed watermark strength.
We also compare against baseline watermarking methods, including KGW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>)</cite> and Unigram <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>)</cite>, both perturb logits over a selected green list. For a fair comparison, we adapt our local edit detection statistic to these settings by treating them as having a degenerate pattern of the form <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="AA\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AA\cdots</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the green list.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the proposed method can detect various post-generation edits with high accuracy, especially when using the longer combinatorial pattern. The detection accuracy generally increases quickly with the edit span, indicating that consecutive edits are easier to detect. Combinatorial patterns significantly outperform baseline KGW and Unigram watermarking methods, especially for detecting deletion-type edits and short-span edits (a particularly challenging case). This is very promising given the simplicity of the proposed edit detection scheme. For a simple pattern with a period of two (the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AB</annotation></semantics></math> pattern used here), it is hard to detect deletions that align exactly with the pattern (e.g., removals of length two, four, and six in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). However, the longer combinatorial pattern can achieve high detection accuracy for such token deletion edits. See Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 More Numerical Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Algorithmic Details and Experimental Results ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> for additional results under varying watermarking strengths, patterns, and sampling mechanisms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results on Watermark Detection</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate watermark detectability, we generate both unwatermarked and watermarked texts of 64 tokens long, on LLaMA-2-7b and OPT-1.3b. We then apply the watermark detection statistics (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.2 Watermark Detection ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) to distinguish between watermarked and unwatermarked texts. The detection threshold is selected to control the Type-I error rate at 0.1, and we report the corresponding Type-II error rate to assess detection effectiveness. Meanwhile, to assess the impact of watermarking on text quality, we compute the perplexity (PPL) of the generated text.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01637v1/Figures/14.PPL.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/165;" width="429" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Tradeoff curve between the Type-II error rate of watermark detection and the perplexity (PPL) of generated text. The red dashed line indicates the perplexity of unwatermarked text.</span></figcaption><img src="2510.01637v1/Figures/14.PPL.legend.png" id="S4.F7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/28;" width="286" height="28" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.2 Results on Watermark Detection ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we plot both the Type-II error rate and PPL across varying watermarking strength <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and different combinatorial patterns. We also include the perplexity of the unwatermarked model for comparison. As the watermark strength <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> increases, we observe a general decrease in the Type-II error rate and an increase in PPL, indicating that the combinatorial watermark becomes more detectable but the generated text is of lower quality. This highlights the fundamental trade-off between watermark detectability and generation quality.
Furthermore, the longer combinatorial pattern—with four unique tags (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B,C,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B,C,D</annotation></semantics></math>)—exhibits the weakest trade-off between detectability and text quality. A possible explanation is that increasing the number of unique tags reduces the size of each sub-vocabulary. This, in turn, degrades text quality and thus increases PPL.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Concluding Remarks</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate and study the problem of local edit detection in watermarked LLM outputs. We introduce a combinatorial pattern-based watermark that embeds rich local structure into the watermarked text.
Leveraging this structure, we derived lightweight statistics that can flag and localize suspect spans containing edits. We evaluate the edit detection performance via experiments across various editing scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are still several limitations of our work. For example, the pattern design space explored is relatively narrow with at most four unique tags, and the method remains less effective for very short edits (one or two tokens), which are challenging to detect. Moreover, we focus on lightweight detection statistics such as (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.2 Watermark Detection ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), which makes minimal assumptions about the underlying pattern. However, the trade-off between watermark detectability, edit detection accuracy, and perplexity could potentially be improved by adopting a more sophisticated detection method. To tackle these challenges, future work includes exploring longer or adaptive pattern designs, further improving the detection accuracy, and extending edit detection to other watermarking frameworks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proofs for Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and More Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<div id="A1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof to Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Type-I error rate of edit detection). ‣ 3.3 Post-Generation Edit Detection ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that the edit detection statistic</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)=\frac{1}{w}\sum_{i=0}^{w-1}I_{w}(t-i)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)=\frac{1}{w}\sum_{i=0}^{w-1}I_{w}(t-i),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">is the normalized count of sliding windows of length <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> that perfectly match the known tag pattern <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here each indicator <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}(t-i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}(t-i)</annotation></semantics></math> takes value <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(s^{(t-i)},\dots,s^{(t-i+w-1)})\in\mathcal{V}_{T_{1}}\times\cdots\times\mathcal{V}_{T_{w}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s^{(t-i)},\dots,s^{(t-i+w-1)})\in\mathcal{V}_{T_{1}}\times\cdots\times\mathcal{V}_{T_{w}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">i.e., the generated tokens in the window fall entirely in the corresponding subset prescribed by <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, with a slight abuse of notation but for simplicity, we just use <math id="A1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\mathcal{V}_{T_{1}}\cdots\mathcal{V}_{T_{w}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{V}_{T_{1}}\cdots\mathcal{V}_{T_{w}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the pattern enforced to tokens within the current sliding window.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Under a clean watermark, by our assumption and the construction of the watermarking scheme, for each window indicator we have <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[I_{w}(t-i)]=\mu_{1}^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[I_{w}(t-i)]=\mu_{1}^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)]=\mu_{1}^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)]=\mu_{1}^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We consider two cases separately. First, if <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}^{(w)}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}^{(w)}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the so-called hard watermarking regime <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>)</cite> where the token is strictly required to be drawn from the corresponding list. For example, this can happen when we set the watermarking strength parameter <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> to be large. Under this case, we have <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> and thus <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)&lt;\tau_{e}\mid\operatorname{no\ edit}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>no</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>edit</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)&lt;\tau_{e}\mid\operatorname{no\ edit}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies no false alarm.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the soft watermarking regime with <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}^{(w)}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}^{(w)}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, in such cases, the token is more likely to be drawn from the corresponding list but is not guaranteed. Note that the list of indicators <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I_{w}(t-i)\}_{i=0}^{w-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I_{w}(t-i)\}_{i=0}^{w-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is not independent, thus the indicators can be represented by a dependency graph: two indicators are adjacent if their corresponding windows overlap.
For windows with offset <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|i-j|=k&lt;w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|i-j|=k&lt;w</annotation></semantics></math>, the overlap size is <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w-k</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the joint probability is</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[I_{w}(t-i)I_{w}(t-j)]=\mu_{1}^{(w+k)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[I_{w}(t-i)I_{w}(t-j)]=\mu_{1}^{(w+k)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define the <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>-dependent constant <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{(w)}:=\sum_{i,j}\mathbb{E}[I_{w}(t-i)I_{w}(t-j)]=w\mu_{1}^{(w)}+2\sum_{k=1}^{w-1}(w-k)\mu_{1}^{(w+k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{(w)}:=\sum_{i,j}\mathbb{E}[I_{w}(t-i)I_{w}(t-j)]=w\mu_{1}^{(w)}+2\sum_{k=1}^{w-1}(w-k)\mu_{1}^{(w+k)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Janson’s inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>)</cite> states that for <math id="A1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z&lt;\mathbb{E}[\sum_{i=0}^{w-1}I_{w}(t-i)]=w\mu_{1}^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z&lt;\mathbb{E}[\sum_{i=0}^{w-1}I_{w}(t-i)]=w\mu_{1}^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(\sum_{i=0}^{w-1}I_{w}(t-i)&lt;z)\leq\exp\!\left(-\frac{(\mathbb{E}[\sum_{i=0}^{w-1}I_{w}(t-i)]-z)^{2}}{2\Delta^{(w)}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(\sum_{i=0}^{w-1}I_{w}(t-i)&lt;z)\leq\exp\!\left(-\frac{(\mathbb{E}[\sum_{i=0}^{w-1}I_{w}(t-i)]-z)^{2}}{2\Delta^{(w)}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the false alarm event <math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)&lt;\tau_{e}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)&lt;\tau_{e}\}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=0}^{w-1}I_{w}(t-i)&lt;w\tau_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=0}^{w-1}I_{w}(t-i)&lt;w\tau_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus we have</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)&lt;\tau_{e}\mid\text{no edit}]\leq\exp\left(-\frac{w^{2}(\mu_{1}^{(w)}-\tau_{e})^{2}}{2\Delta^{(w)}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mtext>no edit</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)&lt;\tau_{e}\mid\text{no edit}]\leq\exp\left(-\frac{w^{2}(\mu_{1}^{(w)}-\tau_{e})^{2}}{2\Delta^{(w)}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discussion.</span> In practice, the pattern alignment probability <math id="A1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math> is generally tractable and there is no closed-form expression. However, the probability for token-level adherence is given in Lemma E.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. For example, under watermarking parameter <math id="A1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming the two sub-vocabulary sets are of equal size, the probability of drawing a token from the current target subset is lower bounded by <math id="A1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}_{1}:=\frac{\tfrac{1}{2}\alpha}{1+\tfrac{1}{2}(\alpha-1)}\;S\!\left(p,\frac{\tfrac{1}{2}(\alpha-1)}{1+\tfrac{1}{2}(\alpha-1)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.501em;" width="0.501em"><mi>S</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}_{1}:=\frac{\tfrac{1}{2}\alpha}{1+\tfrac{1}{2}(\alpha-1)}\;S\!\left(p,\frac{\tfrac{1}{2}(\alpha-1)}{1+\tfrac{1}{2}(\alpha-1)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="A1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=e^{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=e^{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(p,z):=\sum_{k}\frac{p_{k}}{1+zp_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mfrac><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(p,z):=\sum_{k}\frac{p_{k}}{1+zp_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> represent the next-token probability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that the <math id="A1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>-dependent constant <math id="A1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math> can scale as <math id="A1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(w^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(w^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> in the worst case under strong positive correlations among overlapping windows, leading to a relatively loose upper bound on the false alarm rate. On the other hand, if overlapping windows are independent—so that pattern alignment in one window does not affect another—Hoeffding’s inequality yields a much tighter bound that can decay exponentially with <math id="A1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, however, the exact dependence structure among sliding windows in large language models is intricate and difficult to characterize.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We also note that the analysis on the edit detection accuracy would be much more complicated due to the complication of all possible edits. As an example, in the following, we provide an analysis by assuming that the edit happens in such a way that reduces the pattern alignment probability for each sliding window to <math id="A1.p9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{0}^{(w)}:=\mathbb{P}[I_{w}(t)=1],\forall t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}^{(w)}:=\mathbb{P}[I_{w}(t)=1],\forall t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math> is much smaller than the pattern alignment probability <math id="A1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math> when there is no edits (clean watermark). We can apply Theorem 5 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> to obtain an upper bound for the miss detection probability. Specifically, by Theorem 5 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and under our assumption, we have</p>
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)\geq\tau_{e}\mid\text{edit}]\leq w\exp\left(-\frac{w^{2}(\tau_{e}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})^{2}}{4w(w\mu_{0}^{(w)}-w(\tau_{e}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})/3}\right)=w\exp\left(-\frac{3(\tau_{e}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})^{2}}{4(2\mu_{0}^{(w)}+\tau_{e})}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mtext>edit</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{E}(t)\geq\tau_{e}\mid\text{edit}]\leq w\exp\left(-\frac{w^{2}(\tau_{e}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})^{2}}{4w(w\mu_{0}^{(w)}-w(\tau_{e}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})/3}\right)=w\exp\left(-\frac{3(\tau_{e}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})^{2}}{4(2\mu_{0}^{(w)}+\tau_{e})}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">It should be mentioned that the upper bound may not be very informative due to two reasons. First, it is generally much larger than the lower tail probability, as capturing upper-tail probabilities with overlapping time windows is intrinsically difficult <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>)</cite>. Second, here we are assuming that the edits reduce the pattern alignment probability uniformly for each window to <math id="A1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which could be unrealistic in practice. For example, in many cases, a local token insertion may force <math id="A1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}^{(w)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}^{(w)}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for windows containing the edit, resulting in 100% detection accuracy. Therefore, we rely primarily on the empirical results in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> to demonstrate the effectiveness of edit detection across scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis on watermark detectability.</span> We also provide a watermark detectability error analysis for completeness and to support the design of our combinatorial watermarking. The results show that the detection accuracy converges to 1 as <math id="A1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring reliable detection with sufficiently long watermarked text. Likewise, the Type-I error rate converges to 0 as <math id="A1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, implying that sufficiently long unwatermarked text yields a vanishing false alarm rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A.1</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Watermark detection error rates)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that under a clean watermark, the pattern alignment probability for each window of size <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{1}^{(w)}:=\mathbb{P}[I_{w}(t)=1],\forall t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}^{(w)}:=\mathbb{P}[I_{w}(t)=1],\forall t</annotation></semantics></math>. When there is no watermarking, assume this probability is reduced to <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}^{(w)}&lt;\mu_{1}^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}^{(w)}&lt;\mu_{1}^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume the observed data contains <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> tokens in total, and the window size is <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> in the detection statistics.</span></p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The probability of detecting the watermark, for a given detection threshold </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{D}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, is at least</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\geq\tau_{d})\geq 1-\exp\left(-(T-w+1)\frac{(\mu_{1}^{(w)}-\tau_{d})^{2}}{2w\mu_{1}^{(w)}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\geq\tau_{d})\geq 1-\exp\left(-(T-w+1)\frac{(\mu_{1}^{(w)}-\tau_{d})^{2}}{2w\mu_{1}^{(w)}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Type-I error rate (probability of false alarm) when there is no watermarking is</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\geq\tau_{d}]\leq w\cdot\exp\left(-(T-w+1)\frac{3(\tau_{d}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})^{2}}{4w\cdot(2\mu_{0}^{(w)}+\tau_{d})}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\geq\tau_{d}]\leq w\cdot\exp\left(-(T-w+1)\frac{3(\tau_{d}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})^{2}}{4w\cdot(2\mu_{0}^{(w)}+\tau_{d})}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that the global watermark detection statistic</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}=\frac{1}{T-w+1}\sum_{t=1}^{T-w+1}I_{w}(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}=\frac{1}{T-w+1}\sum_{t=1}^{T-w+1}I_{w}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">is the normalized count of sliding windows of length <math id="A1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> that perfectly match the known tag pattern.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Under the watermarking regime, similar to the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Type-I error rate of edit detection). ‣ 3.3 Post-Generation Edit Detection ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, by our assumption and the construction of the watermarking scheme, for each window indicator we have <math id="A1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[I_{w}(t-i)]=\mu_{1}^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[I_{w}(t-i)]=\mu_{1}^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="A1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}(t)]=\mu_{1}^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}(t)]=\mu_{1}^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Again we define the <math id="A1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,w)</annotation></semantics></math>-dependent constant</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta^{(T,w)}:=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}(i,j):\\
|i-j|&lt;w\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}[I_{w}(i)I_{w}(j)]\leq(T-w+1)\mu_{1}^{(w)}+(T-w+1)(w-1)\mu_{1}^{(w)}=(T-w+1)w\mu_{1}^{(w)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>w</mi><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{(T,w)}:=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}(i,j):\\
|i-j|&lt;w\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}[I_{w}(i)I_{w}(j)]\leq(T-w+1)\mu_{1}^{(w)}+(T-w+1)(w-1)\mu_{1}^{(w)}=(T-w+1)w\mu_{1}^{(w)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then we can apply Janson’s inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>)</cite>, which guarantees for <math id="A1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{d}&lt;\mu_{1}^{(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{d}&lt;\mu_{1}^{(w)}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\leq\tau_{d})=\mathbb{P}(\sum_{t=1}^{T-w+1}I_{w}(t)&lt;(T-w+1)\tau_{d})\leq\exp\left(-(T-w+1)^{2}\frac{(\mu_{1}^{(w)}-\tau_{d})^{2}}{2\Delta^{(T,w)}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\leq\tau_{d})=\mathbb{P}(\sum_{t=1}^{T-w+1}I_{w}(t)&lt;(T-w+1)\tau_{d})\leq\exp\left(-(T-w+1)^{2}\frac{(\mu_{1}^{(w)}-\tau_{d})^{2}}{2\Delta^{(T,w)}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">By substituting the upper bound to <math id="A1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{(T,w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{(T,w)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can further simplify the above inequality as</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\leq\tau_{d})\leq\exp\left(-(T-w+1)\frac{(\mu_{1}^{(w)}-\tau_{d})^{2}}{2w\mu_{1}^{(w)}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\leq\tau_{d})\leq\exp\left(-(T-w+1)\frac{(\mu_{1}^{(w)}-\tau_{d})^{2}}{2w\mu_{1}^{(w)}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the false alarm event <math id="A1.p14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\geq\tau_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\geq\tau_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math> when there is no watermarking, we have</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex15X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\geq\tau_{d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">≥</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}[|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\geq\tau_{d}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq w\cdot\exp\left(-(T-w+1)^{2}\frac{(\tau_{d}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})^{2}}{4w\cdot((T-w+1)\mu_{0}^{(w)}+(T-w+1)(\tau_{d}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})/3)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq w\cdot\exp\left(-(T-w+1)^{2}\frac{(\tau_{d}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})^{2}}{4w\cdot((T-w+1)\mu_{0}^{(w)}+(T-w+1)(\tau_{d}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})/3)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex15Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq w\cdot\exp\left(-(T-w+1)\frac{3(\tau_{d}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})^{2}}{4w\cdot(2\mu_{0}^{(w)}+\tau_{d})}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq w\cdot\exp\left(-(T-w+1)\frac{3(\tau_{d}-\mu_{0}^{(w)})^{2}}{4w\cdot(2\mu_{0}^{(w)}+\tau_{d})}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional Algorithmic Details and Experimental Results</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Concrete Examples of Detection Statistics</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To better illustrate the detection algorithms, we give concrete examples of the constructed watermark detection and edit detection statistics for the two exemplary combinatorial patterns in Example <a href="#S3.Thmexample1" title="Example 3.1 (Alternating Binary Pattern). ‣ 3.1 Watermark Generation based on Combinatorial Patterns ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and Example <a href="#S3.Thmexample2" title="Example 3.2 (Alternating Quaternary Pattern). ‣ 3.1 Watermark Generation based on Combinatorial Patterns ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Example <a href="#S3.Thmexample1" title="Example 3.1 (Alternating Binary Pattern). ‣ 3.1 Watermark Generation based on Combinatorial Patterns ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With two unique tags (e.g., <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>), we define the pattern <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}=\{A,B\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}=\{A,B\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus the watermark is governed by the order <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B,A,B,\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B,A,B,\ldots</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as the green and red lists (see Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Notation and Basics ‣ 2 Preliminaries and Problem Setup ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), respectively, aligning with standard terminology in prior work. In the following, with a slight abuse of notation, we use <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> to also denote their corresponding subset of vocabulary, when not causing confusion.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the definition in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 Watermark Detection ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we have the following concrete formulations for <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the core component in our watermark detection and edit detection statistics.</p>
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For window size <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}(t)=\mathds{1}\left\{\text{$s^{(t)}$ and $s^{(t+1)}$ belongs to different sets ($A,B$ or $B,A$)}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mtext> and </mtext><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mtext> belongs to different sets (</mtext><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mtext> or </mtext><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}(t)=\mathds{1}\left\{\text{$s^{(t)}$ and $s^{(t+1)}$ belongs to different sets ($A,B$ or $B,A$)}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For window size <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=4</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="A2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}(t)=\mathds{1}\left\{\text{$s^{(t)},s^{(t+1)},s^{(t+2)},s^{(t+3)}$ are in sets $A,B,A,B$ or $B,A,B,A$}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mtext> are in sets </mtext><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mtext> or </mtext><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}(t)=\mathds{1}\left\{\text{$s^{(t)},s^{(t+1)},s^{(t+2)},s^{(t+3)}$ are in sets $A,B,A,B$ or $B,A,B,A$}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This also illustrates the case when the window size <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds the pattern period.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Example <a href="#S3.Thmexample2" title="Example 3.2 (Alternating Quaternary Pattern). ‣ 3.1 Watermark Generation based on Combinatorial Patterns ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With four unique tags (e.g., <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B,C,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B,C,D</annotation></semantics></math>), we define the combinatorial pattern <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}=\{A,C,A,D,B,C,B,D\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}=\{A,C,A,D,B,C,B,D\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the watermark is governed by the order:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A,C,A,D,B,C,B,D,A,C,A,D,B,C,B,D,\ldots." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A,C,A,D,B,C,B,D,A,C,A,D,B,C,B,D,\ldots.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, we have the following concrete formulation of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> that can be efficiently computed for performing watermark detection and edit detection:</p>
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For window size <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="A2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}(t)=\mathds{1}\left\{\text{$s^{(t)}$ and $s^{(t+1)}$ are in $AC$, $CA$, $AD$, $DB$, $BC$, $CB$, $BD$, or $DA$}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mtext> and </mtext><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mtext> are in </mtext><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mtext>, or </mtext><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}(t)=\mathds{1}\left\{\text{$s^{(t)}$ and $s^{(t+1)}$ are in $AC$, $CA$, $AD$, $DB$, $BC$, $CB$, $BD$, or $DA$}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For window size <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=4</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="A2.Ex20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex20X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex20X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I_{w}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I_{w}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex20X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathds{1}\big\{\text{$s^{(t:t+3)}$ are in $ACAD$, $CADB$, $ADBC$, $DBCB$,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mtext> are in </mtext><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathds{1}\big\{\text{$s^{(t:t+3)}$ are in $ACAD$, $CADB$, $ADBC$, $DBCB$,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex20Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex20Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{$BCBD$, $CBDA$, $BDAC$, or $DACA$ }\big\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mtext>, or </mtext><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{$BCBD$, $CBDA$, $BDAC$, or $DACA$ }\big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The statistics for larger window sizes are similarly constructed based on the same principle.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Insights for combinatorial pattern design.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the detection statistics for watermarking, we list some insights for designing the combinatorial pattern to enable simple watermark detection. For ease of watermark detection, we may impose the following structural restriction on our patterns: we let both pattern length <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and number of unique tags <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> to be even numbers, and the tag assignment alternates between even and odd indices—meaning <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}=T^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}=T^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> only if <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> are both even or both odd. Both examples <a href="#S3.Thmexample1" title="Example 3.1 (Alternating Binary Pattern). ‣ 3.1 Watermark Generation based on Combinatorial Patterns ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmexample2" title="Example 3.2 (Alternating Quaternary Pattern). ‣ 3.1 Watermark Generation based on Combinatorial Patterns ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> satisfy such a property. This design enables simple and effective detection using the smallest window size <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we define the window indicator for positions <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}(t)=\mathds{1}\left\{\text{$s^{(t)}\in{\mathcal{V}}_{odd}$ and $s^{(t+1)}\in{\mathcal{V}}_{even}$, or $s^{(t)}\in{\mathcal{V}}_{even}$ and $s^{(t+1)}\in{\mathcal{V}}_{odd}$}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mtext>, or </mtext><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}(t)=\mathds{1}\left\{\text{$s^{(t)}\in{\mathcal{V}}_{odd}$ and $s^{(t+1)}\in{\mathcal{V}}_{even}$, or $s^{(t)}\in{\mathcal{V}}_{even}$ and $s^{(t+1)}\in{\mathcal{V}}_{odd}$}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}_{odd}=\cup_{1\leq i\leq r,\,\text{$i$ is odd}}{\mathcal{V}}_{T^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mtext> is odd</mtext></mrow></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}_{odd}=\cup_{1\leq i\leq r,\,\text{$i$ is odd}}{\mathcal{V}}_{T^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}_{even}=\cup_{1\leq i\leq r,\,\text{$i$ is even}}{\mathcal{V}}_{T^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mtext> is even</mtext></mrow></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}_{even}=\cup_{1\leq i\leq r,\,\text{$i$ is even}}{\mathcal{V}}_{T^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In other words, detection could rely only on whether the observed token sequence follows the expected even-odd alternation. This simple test does not rely on the full pattern structure. In contrast, the full pattern sequence provides richer information that can be leveraged to localize specific edit positions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also note that the above watermark detection is performed for black-box LLMs. In practice, when we do have access to the logits information (such as in white-box LLMs), we can instead use the log-likelihood ratio as our edit detection statistics and watermark detection statistics, which will yield more accurate detection results due to the utilization of more information. And this can potentially improve both the edit detection accuracy and the watermark detection accuracy.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>More Numerical Results</h3>

<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results With Varying Watermarking Strength <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, Combinatorial Pattern, and Multinomial Sampling.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present more results on the average edit detection accuracy under a variety of watermarking strengths in Figure <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Results With Varying Watermarking Strength 𝛿, Combinatorial Pattern, and Multinomial Sampling. ‣ B.2 More Numerical Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Algorithmic Details and Experimental Results ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. We also included a new pattern with <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=3</annotation></semantics></math> unique tags. We note that higher watermarking strength increases accuracy in general. We also note that for the longer ACADBCBD combinatorial pattern, it becomes more effective only beyond a certain watermarking strength threshold. This is likely because, under lower watermarking strengths, the generated watermarked text does not reliably adhere to the pattern, thus the edit detection is less effective. As the watermarking strength increases, the watermarked text has better adherence to the pattern, leading to better edit detection performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01637v1/15_llama_delta_comparison.svg" id="A2.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/144;" width="476" height="144"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A2.F8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Detection accuracy vs edit type and length under different watermarking strengths <math id="A2.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. This was generated in a similar fashion to Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> using Llama-2-7b, with 200 samples of 100-token long generated text for each combination of edit type, length, watermarking strength, and pattern. The texts in this case are generated using multinomial sampling where (<span id="A2.F8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">temperature=1.0, top_p=0.8</span>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Influence of Post Edits on Watermark Detection.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our primary goal is to design combinatorial patterns that enable more effective edit localization, it is also important to ensure that the underlying watermark remains reliably detectable. We have demonstrated the watermark detectability in Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.2 Results on Watermark Detection ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> using fully watermarked texts.
In Figure <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Influence of Post Edits on Watermark Detection. ‣ B.2 More Numerical Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Algorithmic Details and Experimental Results ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> below, we present some empirical evidence on the influence of post-generation edits on the magnitude of watermark detection statistics, under varying watermarking strengths.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As expected, the watermark detection statistics decrease after post-generation edits, with larger decreases observed for longer edit lengths and more complex patterns. Moreover, for the simplest combinatorial pattern <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AB</annotation></semantics></math>, the degradation in detection statistics is comparable to—or slightly smaller than—that of the two baseline watermarking approaches. This indicates that the combinatorial pattern-based watermarking maintains at least the same level of robustness to post-generation edits, and may even offer improved resilience in certain scenarios. A more comprehensive analysis of robustness, including theoretical aspects such as detection threshold and sensitivity to different edit types, is left for future work. In the following subsection <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 A Sensitivity Analysis on the Watermark Detectability and Robustness ‣ Appendix B Additional Algorithmic Details and Experimental Results ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>, we present preliminary theoretical insights to illustrate general trends and motivate further study.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01637v1/15_llama_delta_comparison_wm.svg" id="A2.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/176;" width="476" height="176"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A2.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Watermark score impact vs edit type and length under different watermarking strengths <math id="A2.F9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. The y-axis shows the watermark score difference <math id="A2.F9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\tilde{{\mathbf{s}}}|_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\tilde{{\mathbf{s}}}|_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> - <math id="A2.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{{\mathbf{s}}}|_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{{\mathbf{s}}}|_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.F9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.F9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathbf{s}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathbf{s}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the original watermarked text and the edited text, respectively. The negative values on the y-axis indicate the watermark detection statistics decrease after edits</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Meaningful Edit Detection / Misinformation Spoofing Attack.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To test the robustness of our watermark against realistic threats, we simulated a <span id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">targeted misinformation attack</span>. We began by generating 139 texts, each 100 tokens long, using OPT-2.7b, embedded with the AB combinatorial pattern watermark with <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>=4.5.
We then tasked the Gemini API to introduce small but harmful edits to the texts one by one using the following prompt:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="145.02" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 145.02" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,145.02) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;" fill="#BFBFBF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 139.12 C 0 142.38 2.64 145.02 5.91 145.02 L 644.09 145.02 C 647.36 145.02 650 142.38 650 139.12 L 650 5.91 C 650 2.64 647.36 0 644.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 120.91 L 648.03 120.91 L 648.03 5.91 C 648.03 3.73 646.27 1.97 644.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 129.51)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:43.85em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="606.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:43.85em;">
<span id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Gemini-2.5-Flash Prompt</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:43.85em;--ltx-fo-height:6.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="95.32" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 92.63)" width="606.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:43.85em;">
<span id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You are an expert on history, facts, and journalism.
<br class="ltx_break">I will give you a text, and your task is to modify part of it so it gives clear misinformation.
<br class="ltx_break">- Your change MUST significantly alter the meaning of the text.
<br class="ltx_break">- Only change 6 words or less, and leave the rest of the text intact.
<br class="ltx_break">- DO NOT ADD extra formatting, emphasis, punctuation, bolds, or italics.
<br class="ltx_break">- Only respond with the modified text. Nothing else.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A human evaluation of a sample of 50 modified texts found that 90% indeed did contain clear misinformation. Finally, we applied our edit detection algorithm to these adversarially modified texts, and evaluated the edit detection accuracy. Table <a href="#A2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Meaningful Edit Detection / Misinformation Spoofing Attack. ‣ B.2 More Numerical Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Algorithmic Details and Experimental Results ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the detection accuracy results, grouped by the number of tokens edited.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A2.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tokens Edited</span></td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T1.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Edit Detection Accuracy (%)</span></td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T1.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Texts</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T1.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">1</td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">81.4%</td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">59</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T1.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">2</td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">90.9%</td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">44</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T1.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">3-9</td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">91.7%</td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">36</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="A2.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">AB (Combinatorial Pattern): Edit detection accuracy under a misinformation attack. The table buckets texts by the number of "Tokens Edited", reports how many "Texts" fall under each bucket, and the corresponding "Edit Detection Accuracy".</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This trend is consistent with our random edit simulations: our algorithm is able to detect and localize edits, and its accuracy increases when more consecutive tokens are modified.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>A Sensitivity Analysis on the Watermark Detectability and Robustness</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide some analysis on the impact of post-generation edits on the watermark detection statistic. In general, the added edits will decrease the watermark detection statistics, as demonstrated in Figure <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Influence of Post Edits on Watermark Detection. ‣ B.2 More Numerical Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Algorithmic Details and Experimental Results ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, thus decreasing the watermark detectability.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall the watermark detector</p>
<table id="A2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\;=\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{t=1}^{N}I_{w}(t),\quad N=T-w+1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\;=\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{t=1}^{N}I_{w}(t),\quad N=T-w+1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is the sliding-window length, <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}(t)\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}(t)\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates whether the window
<math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s^{(t:t+w-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{(t:t+w-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> matches the watermark pattern, <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the text length, and let
<math id="A2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\sum_{t}I_{w}(t)=N\cdot|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\sum_{t}I_{w}(t)=N\cdot|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be the total number of windows that match the pattern.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a token at absolute position <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\{1,\dots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\{1,\dots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>
windows whose <em id="A2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">starting</em> indices lie in</p>
<table id="A2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl\{\,u-w+1,\,u-w+2,\,\dots,\,u\,\bigr\}\cap[N]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\{\,u-w+1,\,u-w+2,\,\dots,\,u\,\bigr\}\cap[N].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Hence <em id="A2.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any single-token perturbation can flip at most <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> indicators
<math id="A2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, we use <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{ins}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{ins}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{del}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>del</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{del}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{rep}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{rep}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the token numbers in insertions, deletions, replacements. And let <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{I}_{w}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{I}_{w}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the indicator after editing. Meanwhile, we use <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the worst-case loss of the number of matched windows attributable to the corresponding edit type <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math>. We first consider three cases separately.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I3" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Replacements</span>. Replacements alter content but keep length fixed, so <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> remains unchanged. Moreover, each new token can break at most <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> windows. Therefore for
<math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{rep}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{rep}}</annotation></semantics></math> replacements, the worst-case loss of matched windows is <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathrm{rep}}=\min\{wS_{\mathrm{rep}},M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathrm{rep}}=\min\{wS_{\mathrm{rep}},M\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the resulted edited text <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the watermark detection statistics after edits thus become</p>
<table id="A2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\tilde{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\geq\frac{M-\Delta_{\mathrm{rep}}}{N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\tilde{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\geq\frac{M-\Delta_{\mathrm{rep}}}{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Insertions</span>. Adding <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{ins}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{ins}}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens grows length to <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T+S_{\mathrm{ins}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T+S_{\mathrm{ins}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the window count increases from <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N+S_{\mathrm{ins}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N+S_{\mathrm{ins}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For
<math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{ins}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{ins}}</annotation></semantics></math> insertions, we have the worst-case loss of matched windows is <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathrm{ins}}\;=\;\min\{\,wS_{\mathrm{ins}},\,M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathrm{ins}}\;=\;\min\{\,wS_{\mathrm{ins}},\,M\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the resulted edited text <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, this yields</p>
<table id="A2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\tilde{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\;\geq\;\frac{M-\Delta_{\mathrm{ins}}}{N+S_{\mathrm{ins}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\tilde{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\;\geq\;\frac{M-\Delta_{\mathrm{ins}}}{N+S_{\mathrm{ins}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{ins}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{ins}}</annotation></semantics></math> appears in the denominator, the worst-case statistics after insertions degrade <em id="A2.I3.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">faster</em> than
replacements (which leave <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deletions</span>.
Removing <math id="A2.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{del}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>del</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{del}}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens shortens the text, so the window count decreases from <math id="A2.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N-S_{\mathrm{del}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>del</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N-S_{\mathrm{del}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Again at most <math id="A2.I3.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> of the indicators <math id="A2.I3.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> can flip, giving <math id="A2.I3.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathrm{del}}=\min\{wS_{\mathrm{del}},M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>del</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>del</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathrm{del}}=\min\{wS_{\mathrm{del}},M\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The watermark detection statistics after edits thus satisfy</p>
<table id="A2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\tilde{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\;\geq\;\frac{M-\Delta_{\mathrm{del}}}{N-S_{\mathrm{del}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>del</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>del</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\tilde{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}\;\geq\;\frac{M-\Delta_{\mathrm{del}}}{N-S_{\mathrm{del}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To summarize, each single-token edit can disrupt at most <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> windows as shown in the following table.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A2.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.SS3.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.SS3.p7.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Edit type</span></th>
<th id="A2.SS3.p7.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.SS3.p7.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lost matches</span></th>
<th id="A2.SS3.p7.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.SS3.p7.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Window count change</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.SS3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.SS3.p7.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Insertion</th>
<td id="A2.SS3.p7.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq w</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.SS3.p7.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.SS3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.SS3.p7.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.SS3.p7.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Deletion</th>
<td id="A2.SS3.p7.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.SS3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq w</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.SS3.p7.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.SS3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.SS3.p7.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.SS3.p7.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Replacement</th>
<td id="A2.SS3.p7.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="A2.SS3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq w</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.SS3.p7.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="A2.SS3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Collecting the individual effects and clipping at zero yields the
deterministic worst-case bound</p>
<table id="A2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathbf{s}}_{\text{edited}}|_{D}\;\geq\;\frac{M-w\bigl(S_{\mathrm{ins}}+S_{\mathrm{del}}+S_{\mathrm{rep}}\bigr)}{N+S_{\mathrm{ins}}-S_{\mathrm{del}}},\," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mtext>edited</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>del</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>del</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathbf{s}}_{\text{edited}}|_{D}\;\geq\;\frac{M-w\bigl(S_{\mathrm{ins}}+S_{\mathrm{del}}+S_{\mathrm{rep}}\bigr)}{N+S_{\mathrm{ins}}-S_{\mathrm{del}}},\,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">here <math id="A2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{s}}_{\text{edited}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mtext>edited</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{s}}_{\text{edited}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the resulting text after all edits.
The numerator loses up to <math id="A2.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> matches per corrupted token; the
denominator is stretched by insertions and contracted by deletions,
remaining unchanged for replacements.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpreting the bound</span>. From the worst-case lower bound in (<a href="#A2.E6" title="In B.3 A Sensitivity Analysis on the Watermark Detectability and Robustness ‣ Appendix B Additional Algorithmic Details and Experimental Results ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), it can be seen that if one wishes to tolerate at most <math id="A2.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(S_{\mathrm{ins}},S_{\mathrm{del}},S_{\mathrm{rep}}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>del</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(S_{\mathrm{ins}},S_{\mathrm{del}},S_{\mathrm{rep}}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> benign edits, we can plug those values into (<a href="#A2.E6" title="In B.3 A Sensitivity Analysis on the Watermark Detectability and Robustness ‣ Appendix B Additional Algorithmic Details and Experimental Results ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and set the decision threshold <math id="A2.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> no greater than the resulting lower bound. This guarantees that if the watermark is detectable before the edit, then it can also be detected after <math id="A2.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(S_{\mathrm{ins}},S_{\mathrm{del}},S_{\mathrm{rep}}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ins</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>del</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(S_{\mathrm{ins}},S_{\mathrm{del}},S_{\mathrm{rep}}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> edits.
Moreover, since a single insertion, deletion, or replacement can disrupt at most <math id="A2.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> matching windows, the worst-case degradation (<math id="A2.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}-|{\mathbf{s}}_{\text{edited}}|_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mtext>edited</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathbf{s}}|_{D}-|{\mathbf{s}}_{\text{edited}}|_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>) grows linearly with <math id="A2.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus a smaller window size yields smaller worst-case degradation. However, a smaller window size might be less effective in detecting edits, so there exists some tradeoff in window size selection, and we generally use a larger window for longer patterns in this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Complexity Analysis</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be the length of the text in tokens and <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> be the length of the sliding window for detection. The complexity of our detection metrics, defined in (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.2 Watermark Detection ‣ 3 Combinatorial Pattern-based Watermarking for Edit Detection ‣ Detecting Post-generation Edits to Watermarked LLM Outputs via Combinatorial Watermarking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), is as follows. First, we analyze the complexity of the naive implementation.</p>
<ul id="A3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single Window Score Complexity</span> <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(I_{w}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(I_{w}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>: To calculate the score for a single window at position <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we compare the token window of length <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> against all <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> possible cyclic shifts of the watermark pattern. Each comparison involves <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> token-wise operations, taking <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(w)</annotation></semantics></math> time. Therefore, the total time complexity for one window is <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(w^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(w^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detection Score Complexity</span> <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(|\mathcal{S}|_{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(|\mathcal{S}|_{D})</annotation></semantics></math>: This score requires computing <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> for every possible window in the text. There are <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-w+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-w+1</annotation></semantics></math> such windows. The total complexity is thus <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T)\cdot O(w^{2})=O(Tw^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T)\cdot O(w^{2})=O(Tw^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Edit Score Complexity</span> <math id="A3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(|\mathcal{S}|_{E}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(|\mathcal{S}|_{E}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>: The edit score at a position <math id="A3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> requires <math id="A3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> evaluations of the <math id="A3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> function. The complexity is therefore <math id="A3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\cdot O(w^{2})=O(w^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\cdot O(w^{2})=O(w^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. To compute this for all <math id="A3.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> positions in the text, the total complexity becomes <math id="A3.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(Tw^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(Tw^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The naive approach can be optimized using techniques such as rolling hashes. A rolling hash allows the hash of a new window (e.g., from token <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to token <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math>) to be calculated in <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> time from the previous window’s hash. This would reduce the <span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">amortized</span> complexity of computing a window’s hash to <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then using a hash-table, we can look up if there are any matching shifts in <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the complexities would become:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f(I_{w}(t))=O(1),\quad f(|\mathcal{S}|_{D})=O(T),\quad\text{and}\quad f(\{|\mathcal{S}|_{E}(t)\}_{t=1}^{T})=O(Tw)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(I_{w}(t))=O(1),\quad f(|\mathcal{S}|_{D})=O(T),\quad\text{and}\quad f(\{|\mathcal{S}|_{E}(t)\}_{t=1}^{T})=O(Tw).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we use the naive implementation, as the window size <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is small and fixed in all our experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
